WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:17.072
"Here is sixty-thousand dollars," said Mr. Fogg, emptying his carpetbag almost to the last of his money.

2
00:00:17.073 --> 00:00:23.164
The Henrietta was a fine ship but twenty years old, and this price was easily double its worth.

3
00:00:23.165 --> 00:00:28.319
The ship's deck and cabins were made of wood, but its hull was of iron.

4
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.929
"I will keep the iron hull and the engine," said Captain Speedy.

5
00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.008
He seized the bank notes and counted them.

6
00:00:36.009 --> 00:00:40.978
Mr. Fogg's first command as the Henrietta's new owner was to pull the boat apart.

7
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:46.646
The rails, bunks, masts, and life rafts were immediately fed into the boilers.

8
00:00:46.647 --> 00:00:55.084
The next day, with Passepartout joining in, the crew pulled down the bridge and the cabins and began to tear up the very deck.

9
00:00:55.085 --> 00:01:01.403
On December 20, as it approached the coast of Ireland, the Henrietta was nothing more than a flat hulk.

10
00:01:01.404 --> 00:01:09.899
By ten o'clock in the evening, the last possible sliver of wood had gone into the fire, but the boat was still 24 hours from Liverpool.

11
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.952
"Everything is against you," Captain Speedy said to Mr. Fogg.

12
00:01:13.953 --> 00:01:16.968
"You have only reached the harbor of Queenstown."

13
00:01:16.969 --> 00:01:22.551
Mr. Fogg knew that Queenstown was the Irish port where the transatlantic steamers dropped off the mail.

14
00:01:22.552 --> 00:01:28.233
Trains carried the mail to Dublin, and from there fast boats delivered it to Liverpool.

15
00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:31.962
This way the mail arrived 12 hours ahead of the steamers.

16
00:01:31.963 --> 00:01:38.351
Relying on the Henrietta's last puffs of steam, Mr. Fogg directed the boat toward the harbor lights on the shore.

17
00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:42.322
The Henrietta entered Queenstown at one o'clock in the morning.

18
00:01:42.323 --> 00:01:49.676
Phileas Fogg, leaving Captain Speedy on the iron hulk of his boat, went ashore with his party at once.

19
00:01:49.677 --> 00:01:57.032
They took the express train to Dublin and then crossed the Irish Sea, arriving in Liverpool at noon on December 21.

20
00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:01.962
He was only six hours distant from London. The wager would be won!

21
00:02:01.963 --> 00:02:07.669
At that moment Fix stepped up beside Mr. Fogg, put his hand on his shoulder, and showing the warrant, said,

22
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:12.177
"I arrest you, Phileas Fogg, in the Queen's name!"

23
00:02:12.178 --> 00:02:15.319
Ten minutes later Phileas Fogg was in prison.

24
00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:21.220
Aouda and Passepartout sat in the waiting room of the police station, not willing to leave.

25
00:02:21.221 --> 00:02:28.985
Passepartout, when he had seen his master arrested, had wanted to attack Fix, but a second policeman had held him back.

26
00:02:28.986 --> 00:02:32.092
Aouda was thunderstruck at the sudden event.

27
00:02:32.093 --> 00:02:39.209
When Passepartout explained the situation to her, her heart refused to believe the wicked charge against her protector.

28
00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:45.808
Realizing that Mr. Fogg was now in need of saving and she could do nothing to help, Aouda cried bitterly.

29
00:02:45.809 --> 00:02:48.837
Passepartout cried as well, hanging his head.

30
00:02:48.838 --> 00:02:53.456
"If only I had not concealed Fix's errand," he said bitterly.

31
00:02:53.457 --> 00:02:59.428
"If I had revealed Fix's true purpose, Mr. Fogg could have proven his innocence long before today!"

32
00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:03.986
As for Mr. Fix, he had arrested Mr. Fogg because it was his duty.

33
00:03:03.987 --> 00:03:09.071
The matter was simple. He was a police detective with a warrant for Mr. Fogg's arrest.

34
00:03:09.072 --> 00:03:12.567
Mr. Fogg's guilt or innocence was not his concern.

35
00:03:12.568 --> 00:03:17.896
The arrested man sat in his cell, motionless, calm, and without apparent anger.

36
00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:23.202
He carefully removed his watch, set it on the table, and observed its advancing hands.

37
00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:28.528
The door was locked and the window heavily barred. The situation was terrible.

38
00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:33.419
If Phileas Fogg was honest, he was ruined; and if he was a thief, he was caught!

39
00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:41.371
He pulled his journal and pen from his pocket and wrote, "December 21, Saturday, Liverpool, 80th day, 12:20 p.m."

40
00:03:41.372 --> 00:03:45.072
He waited, but for what? Did he still hold out hope?

41
00:03:45.073 --> 00:03:49.126
Was he planning an escape? No one could tell.

42
00:03:49.127 --> 00:03:52.262
Two hours passed, and then half an hour more.

43
00:03:52.263 --> 00:03:57.568
He heard a noise outside—quick steps, and then a hasty opening of doors.

44
00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:02.404
Passepartout's voice was audible and immediately after, so was the voice of Fix.

45
00:04:02.405 --> 00:04:08.551
The cell door swung open, and Passepartout, Aouda, and Fix hurried toward Fogg.

46
00:04:08.552 --> 00:04:15.428
Fix was out of breath. "Sir, forgive me . . . most . . . unfortunate resemblance . . .

47
00:04:15.429 --> 00:04:20.510
The true robber was arrested three days ago. You are free!"

48
00:04:20.511 --> 00:04:24.956
Phileas Fogg walked up to the detective and looked him steadily in the face.

49
00:04:24.957 --> 00:04:32.746
With the only rapid motion he ever made in his life, he drew back his arm and knocked Fix down with one precise punch.

50
00:04:32.747 --> 00:04:36.310
"Well hit!" cried Passepartout.

51
00:04:36.311 --> 00:04:43.198
Phileas Fogg was free! It was just after three o'clock, and Mr. Fogg was due at the Reform Club at a quarter to nine.

52
00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:46.808
They would have to make the journey to London in five and a half hours.

53
00:04:46.809 --> 00:04:54.438
Mr. Fogg immediately ordered a special train and set out for London with Aouda and his faithful servant.

54
00:04:54.439 --> 00:04:57.689
Their last hope was to keep the train at top speed.

55
00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:02.515
This would have been easy if the rails had been clear, but other trains caused delays.

56
00:05:02.516 --> 00:05:08.955
So when Mr. Fogg stepped from his train at the station, all the clocks in London were striking ten minutes before nine.

57
00:05:08.956 --> 00:05:19.887
Having completed his tour of the world, he was five minutes too late! He had lost his wager.

58
00:05:19.888 --> 00:05:23.820
Can you believe it? I can't. I'm still in shock!

59
00:05:23.821 --> 00:05:28.888
After all that, Mr. Fogg lost his wager—and by only five minutes.

60
00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:33.979
If only we had gone faster. Although at the end of our trip, we rushed!

61
00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:38.182
Queenstown to Liverpool to London in just over a day!

62
00:05:38.183 --> 00:05:41.413
Queenstown is a port on the southern coast of Ireland.

63
00:05:41.414 --> 00:05:45.036
This city has had several names, including Cove and Cobh.

64
00:05:45.037 --> 00:05:50.406
It was officially renamed Queenstown about 25 years ago, after Queen Victoria visited.

65
00:05:50.407 --> 00:05:55.645
Some people think that someday the city will go back to its original name, Cobh.

66
00:05:55.646 --> 00:06:00.398
Liverpool, located in northwest England, is also a major seaport.

67
00:06:00.399 --> 00:06:06.776
In fact, it is sometimes called "the second port of the Empire" because it is the busiest port after London.

68
00:06:06.777 --> 00:06:14.248
There are many beautiful buildings here, displaying this city's impressive wealth.
